INTERPRETATION

A38 is not asking
whether the organism should remember more.

It asks whether
all cells should remember
on the same timescale.

The persistent failure environments
contain a strong long-run directional signal
interrupted by short counter-pulses.

If every cell responds
on nearly the same timescale,
those counter-pulses can move
the whole population together.

A mixed population could instead contain:

fast cells
that notice change immediately;

medium cells
that require more persistence;

slow cells
that preserve the longer-run state.

The population then carries
multiple temporal hypotheses simultaneously.

PLAIN-SPEAK QUESTION

The stubborn worlds are not evenly confused.

Most of the time
they say:

"CANDIDATE."

Then for a short burst
they say:

"STALE."

Then candidate returns.

Yggdrasil keeps letting
too much of the population
chase that short burst.

A38 gives different cells
different reaction speeds.

Some are fast.

Some are medium.

Some are slow.

The fast cells can still react
if the world really changes.

The slow cells can keep
the longer history alive.

The question is:

DOES A POPULATION BECOME MORE STABLE
WHEN ITS CELLS DO NOT ALL
LIVE ON THE SAME CLOCK?

